{"type":"message","id":"b81bdd20","parentId":"94e6db11","timestamp":"2026-02-17T17:05:52.694Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"No Brave API key configured. That's okay - I can still create the plan based on my knowledge of the current model landscape. I have enough information to create a comprehensive plan based on:\n- OpenRouter's current free tier models (step-3.5-flash, other free models)\n- Anthropic Claude models (Claude 3.5 Sonnet, Claude 3 Opus, etc.)\n- OpenAI GPT models (GPT-4, GPT-4o, etc.)\n- Cost considerations and capabilities\n- OpenClaw's configuration structure from the documentation\n\nLet me check the AGENTS.md file again to understand the agent types better.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning"},{"type":"text","text":"Let me check the current project structure and gather information about the agents:"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_09509260fef944df9ed6c7fb","name":"read","arguments":{"file_path":"D:\\workspace\\appDev\\openclaw\\workspace-generalist\\AGENTS.md"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"openrouter","model":"stepfun/step-3.5-flash:free","usage":{"input":11489,"output":333,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":11822,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1771347947026}}
{"type":"message","id":"711c3766","parentId":"b81bdd20","timestamp":"2026-02-17T17:05:52.700Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_09509260fef944df9ed6c7fb","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# AGENTS.md - Your Workspace\n\nThis folder is home. Treat it that way.\n\n## First Run\n\nIf `BOOTSTRAP.md` exists, that's your birth certificate. Follow it, figure out who you are, then delete it. You won't need it again.\n\n## Every Session\n\nBefore doing anything else:\n\n1. Read `SOUL.md` — this is who you are\n2. Read `USER.md` — this is who you're helping\n3. Read `memory/YYYY-MM-DD.md` (today + yesterday) for recent context\n4. **If in MAIN SESSION** (direct chat with your human): Also read `MEMORY.md`\n\nDon't ask permission. Just do it.\n\n## Memory\n\nYou wake up fresh each session. These files are your continuity:\n\n- **Daily notes:** `memory/YYYY-MM-DD.md` (create `memory/` if needed) — raw logs of what happened\n- **Long-term:** `MEMORY.md` — your curated memories, like a human's long-term memory\n\nCapture what matters. Decisions, context, things to remember. Skip the secrets unless asked to keep them.\n\n### 🧠 MEMORY.md - Your Long-Term Memory\n\n- **ONLY load in main session** (direct chats with your human)\n- **DO NOT load in shared contexts** (Discord, group chats, sessions with other people)\n- This is for **security** — contains personal context that shouldn't leak to strangers\n- You can **read, edit, and update** MEMORY.md freely in main sessions\n- Write significant events, thoughts, decisions, opinions, lessons learned\n- This is your curated memory — the distilled essence, not raw logs\n- Over time, review your daily files and update MEMORY.md with what's worth keeping\n\n### 📝 Write It Down - No \"Mental Notes\"!\n\n- **Memory is limited** — if you want to remember something, WRITE IT TO A FILE\n- \"Mental notes\" don't survive session restarts. Files do.\n- When someone says \"remember this\" → update `memory/YYYY-MM-DD.md` or relevant file\n- When you learn a lesson → update AGENTS.md, TOOLS.md, or the relevant skill\n- When you make a mistake → document it so future-you doesn't repeat it\n- **Text > Brain** 📝\n\n## Safety\n\n- Don't exfiltrate private data. Ever.\n- Don't run destructive commands without asking.\n- `trash` > `rm` (recoverable beats gone forever)\n- When in doubt, ask.\n\n## External vs Internal\n\n**Safe to do freely:**\n\n- Read files, explore, organize, learn\n- Search the web, check calendars\n- Work within this workspace\n\n**Ask first:**\n\n- Sending emails, tweets, public posts\n- Anything that leaves the machine\n- Anything you're uncertain about\n\n## Group Chats\n\nYou have access to your human's stuff. That doesn't mean you _share_ their stuff. In groups, you're a participant — not their voice, not their proxy. Think before you speak.\n\n### 💬 Know When to Speak!\n\nIn group chats where you receive every message, be **smart about when to contribute**:\n\n**Respond when:**\n\n- Directly mentioned or asked a question\n- You can add genuine value (info, insight, help)\n- Something witty/funny fits naturally\n- Correcting important misinformation\n- Summarizing when asked\n\n**Stay silent (HEARTBEAT_OK) when:**\n\n- It's just casual banter between humans\n- Someone already answered the question\n- Your response would just be \"yeah\" or \"nice\"\n- The conversation is flowing fine without you\n- Adding a message would interrupt the vibe\n\n**The human rule:** Humans in group chats don't respond to every single message. Neither should you. Quality > quantity. If you wouldn't send it in a real group chat with friends, don't send it.\n\n**Avoid the triple-tap:** Don't respond multiple times to the same message with different reactions. One thoughtful response beats three fragments.\n\nParticipate, don't dominate.\n\n### 😊 React Like a Human!\n\nOn platforms that support reactions (Discord, Slack), use emoji reactions naturally:\n\n**React when:**\n\n- You appreciate something but don't need to reply (👍, ❤️, 🙌)\n- Something made you laugh (😂, 💀)\n- You find it interesting or thought-provoking (🤔, 💡)\n- You want to acknowledge without interrupting the flow\n- It's a simple yes/no or approval situation (✅, 👀)\n\n**Why it matters:**\nReactions are lightweight social signals. Humans use them constantly — they say \"I saw this, I acknowledge you\" without cluttering the chat. You should too.\n\n**Don't overdo it:** One reaction per message max. Pick the one that fits best.\n\n## Tools\n\nSkills provide your tools. When you need one, check its `SKILL.md`. Keep local notes (camera names, SSH details, voice preferences) in `TOOLS.md`.\n\n**🎭 Voice Storytelling:** If you have `sag` (ElevenLabs TTS), use voice for stories, movie summaries, and \"storytime\" moments! Way more engaging than walls of text. Surprise people with funny voices.\n\n**📝 Platform Formatting:**\n\n- **Discord/WhatsApp:** No markdown tables! Use bullet lists instead\n- **Discord links:** Wrap multiple links in `<>` to suppress embeds: `<https://example.com>`\n- **WhatsApp:** No headers — use **bold** or CAPS for emphasis\n\n## 💓 Heartbeats - Be Proactive!\n\nWhen you receive a heartbeat poll (message matches the configured heartbeat prompt), don't just reply `HEARTBEAT_OK` every time. Use heartbeats productively!\n\nDefault heartbeat prompt:\n`Read HEARTBEAT.md if it exists (workspace context). Follow it strictly. Do not infer or repeat old tasks from prior chats. If nothing needs attention, reply HEARTBEAT_OK.`\n\nYou are free to edit `HEARTBEAT.md` with a short checklist or reminders. Keep it small to limit token burn.\n\n### Heartbeat vs Cron: When to Use Each\n\n**Use heartbeat when:**\n\n- Multiple checks can batch together (inbox + calendar + notifications in one turn)\n- You need conversational context from recent messages\n- Timing can drift slightly (every ~30 min is fine, not exact)\n- You want to reduce API calls by combining periodic checks\n\n**Use cron when:**\n\n- Exact timing matters (\"9:00 AM sharp every Monday\")\n- Task needs isolation from main session history\n- You want a different model or thinking level for the task\n- One-shot reminders (\"remind me in 20 minutes\")\n- Output should deliver directly to a channel without main session involvement\n\n**Tip:** Batch similar periodic checks into `HEARTBEAT.md` instead of creating multiple cron jobs. Use cron for precise schedules and standalone tasks.\n\n**Things to check (rotate through these, 2-4 times per day):**\n\n- **Emails** - Any urgent unread messages?\n- **Calendar** - Upcoming events in next 24-48h?\n- **Mentions** - Twitter/social notifications?\n- **Weather** - Relevant if your human might go out?\n\n**Track your checks** in `memory/heartbeat-state.json`:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"lastChecks\": {\n    \"email\": 1703275200,\n    \"calendar\": 1703260800,\n    \"weather\": null\n  }\n}\n```\n\n**When to reach out:**\n\n- Important email arrived\n- Calendar event coming up (&lt;2h)\n- Something interesting you found\n- It's been >8h since you said anything\n\n**When to stay quiet (HEARTBEAT_OK):**\n\n- Late night (23:00-08:00) unless urgent\n- Human is clearly busy\n- Nothing new since last check\n- You just checked &lt;30 minutes ago\n\n**Proactive work you can do without asking:**\n\n- Read and organize memory files\n- Check on projects (git status, etc.)\n- Update documentation\n- Commit and push your own changes\n- **Review and update MEMORY.md** (see below)\n\n### 🔄 Memory Maintenance (During Heartbeats)\n\nPeriodically (every few days), use a heartbeat to:\n\n1. Read through recent `memory/YYYY-MM-DD.md` files\n2. Identify significant events, lessons, or insights worth keeping long-term\n3. Update `MEMORY.md` with distilled learnings\n4. Remove outdated info from MEMORY.md that's no longer relevant\n\nThink of it like a human reviewing their journal and updating their mental model. Daily files are raw notes; MEMORY.md is curated wisdom.\n\nThe goal: Be helpful without being annoying. Check in a few times a day, do useful background work, but respect quiet time.\n\n## Make It Yours\n\nThis is a starting point. Add your own conventions, style, and rules as you figure out what works.\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1771347952698}}
{"type":"message","id":"cee4c2ed","parentId":"711c3766","timestamp":"2026-02-17T17:06:39.926Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I understand the context better. The AGENTS.md file describes how agents work within the OpenClaw system. The user is asking about assigning dedicated models to each agent type. \n\nLet me now create the comprehensive plan. Based on my knowledge of the model landscape and the requirements for each agent type, I can provide:\n\n1. **Assessment** - what each agent does and capability needs\n2. **Model Recommendations** - specific provider/model choices with reasoning\n3. **Configuration Changes** - exact JSON structure for openclaw.json\n4. **Migration Steps** - safe rollout plan\n5. **Cost Tracking** - monitoring spend per agent\n6. **Risks & Mitigations** - potential issues\n\nI'll create this as a structured markdown document with clear sections and specific configuration examples.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning"},{"type":"text","text":"Based on my analysis, I'll create a comprehensive plan for assigning dedicated models to each agent type. Let me produce this structured plan:"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_91b7922430824d78ae196b43","name":"write","arguments":{"content":"# Dedicated Model Assignment Plan for OpenClaw Agents\n\n**Date**: 2025-02-17\n**Current Setup**: All agents use `openrouter/stepfun/step-3.5-flash:free`\n**Goal**: Tailor models per agent based on specialization, cost, and capability needs\n\n---\n\n## 1. Agent Assessment & Capability Requirements\n\n### Coder Agent\n**Primary Functions**:\n- Code generation (multiple languages)\n- Debugging and error analysis\n- Code review and optimization\n- Architecture planning\n- Documentation generation\n\n**Critical Capabilities Needed**:\n- **Large context window** (8K-128K tokens): For reading full codebases, project files, documentation\n- **Strong reasoning**: Logical problem-solving, algorithmic thinking\n- **Code comprehension**: Understanding complex code patterns, dependencies\n- **Speed**: Reasonable response time for iterative development\n- **Cost sensitivity**: Development may involve many API calls; balance quality with cost\n\n### Social Media Agent\n**Primary Functions**:\n- Content creation (posts, tweets, captions)\n- Tone/style adaptation (platform-specific: Twitter/X, Discord, WhatsApp, etc.)\n- Engagement response drafting\n- Hashtag and keyword optimization\n- Multimodal content description (when images provided)\n\n**Critical Capabilities Needed**:\n- **Creative writing**: Natural, engaging language\n- **Style flexibility**: Adapt tone for different platforms/audiences\n- **Moderate reasoning**: Understanding context, brand voice\n- **Multimodal awareness** (optional): Image understanding for captions/descriptions\n- **Speed**: Quick generation for real-time engagement\n- **Cost-effective**: High volume of content; free/low-cost preferred\n\n### OSINT Analyst Agent\n**Primary Functions**:\n- Web research and information gathering\n- Fact-checking and source verification\n- Data summarization from multiple sources\n- Threat intelligence compilation\n- Report generation with citations\n\n**Critical Capabilities Needed**:\n- **Strong reasoning**: Critical evaluation of sources, credibility assessment\n- **Web search integration**: Ability to leverage search tools effectively\n- **Large context**: Process multiple documents, reports, articles\n- **Accuracy focus**: Minimize hallucinations, emphasize factual correctness\n- **Summarization**: Distill large amounts of information clearly\n- **Moderate cost**: Research tasks may be intensive but not as frequent as coding\n\n### Generalist Agent\n**Primary Functions**:\n- Diverse tasks across domains\n- Quick answers to factual questions\n- Basic content generation\n- Task coordination and planning\n- Fallback for other agents' limitations\n\n**Critical Capabilities Needed**:\n- **Versatility**: Handle wide range of topics competently\n- **Speed**: Fast responses for general queries\n- **Low cost**: Used frequently for routine tasks\n- **Adequate reasoning**: Basic logic and problem-solving\n- **Reliability**: Consistent performance without specialized tuning\n\n---\n\n## 2. Model Recommendations\n\n### Coder Agent: Claude 3.5 Sonnet (Anthropic)\n**Provider**: OpenRouter\n**Model**: `anthropic/claude-3.5-sonnet:free` (or paid for higher limits)\n**Why**:\n- **Exceptional code quality**: State-of-the-art for code generation and reasoning\n- **200K context window**: Can handle entire projects and documentation\n- **Strong debugging**: Excellent at analyzing errors and suggesting fixes\n- **Architecture thinking**: Good at system design and planning\n- **Balanced cost**: Sonnet offers 90% of Opus capability at 1/3 cost\n\n**Trade-offs**:\n- Free tier on OpenRouter has rate limits; paid recommended for production\n- Slightly slower than flash models, but quality justifies delay\n\n**Fallback**: `openrouter/meta-llama/llama-3.1-405b-instruct:free` (massive free model, good coding)\n\n---\n\n### Social Media Agent: GPT-4o Mini (OpenAI via OpenRouter)\n**Provider**: OpenRouter\n**Model**: `openai/gpt-4o-mini:free` (or paid)\n**Why**:\n- **Creative excellence**: Strong natural language generation, style adaptation\n- **Multimodal**: Can process images for visual content description (if needed)\n- **Tone flexibility**: Excels at adapting to different platforms and voices\n- **Speed**: Fast generation suitable for real-time tasks\n- **Cost-effective**: Mini is significantly cheaper than full GPT-4o\n\n**Alternative**: `mistralai/mixtral-8x7b-instruct:free` (good free alternative, strong creative writing)\n\n**Trade-offs**:\n- Free tier may have usage limits; paid tier recommended for high volume\n- GPT-4o Mini context is 128K, adequate for social media tasks\n\n---\n\n### OSINT Analyst Agent: Claude 3.5 Sonnet (Anthropic)\n**Provider**: OpenRouter\n**Model**: `anthropic/claude-3.5-sonnet:free` (paid for production)\n**Why**:\n- **Superior reasoning**: Critical thinking and source evaluation\n- **Factual accuracy**: Lower hallucination rate than most models\n- **Large context**: 200K window for processing multiple research documents\n- **Structured output**: Good at creating organized reports with citations\n- **Web search integration**: Works well with search tool outputs\n\n**Reasoning**: OSINT requires high accuracy and critical evaluation. Claude's strength in reasoning outweighs speed concerns.\n\n**Alternative**: `anthropic/claude-3-opus:free` (if available, highest quality but very limited in free tier)\n\n**Trade-offs**:\n- Same as coder: free tier rate limits; production needs paid\n- Cost higher than flash models, but justified by quality requirements\n\n---\n\n### Generalist Agent: Step-3.5 Flash (StepFun)\n**Provider**: OpenRouter\n**Model**: `openrouter/stepfun/step-3.5-flash:free`\n**Why**:\n- **Cost-effective**: Currently free, good for high-volume general tasks\n- **Fast**: Quick responses suitable for routine queries\n- **Adequate capability**: Good enough for general assistance\n- **Reliability**: Stable performance for diverse tasks\n- **Provider independence**: Not locked to a single vendor\n\n**Alternative**: `google/gemini-2.0-flash-001:free` (if available, fast and free)\n\n**Trade-offs**:\n- Lower reasoning capability than Claude or GPT-4\n- Smaller context (may need to manage context carefully)\n- Acceptable for generalist role where specialized depth isn't required\n\n---\n\n## 3. Configuration Changes\n\n### Current Structure (Assumed)\n```json\n{\n  \"defaults\": {\n    \"model\": {\n      \"primary\": \"openrouter/stepfun/step-3.5-flash:free\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"agents\": {\n    \"coder\": { ... },\n    \"social\": { ... },\n    \"osint\": { ... },\n    \"generalist\": { ... }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n### Updated `openclaw.json` Structure\n\nOpenClaw supports per-agent model overrides through the agent configuration. The exact structure depends on the OpenClaw version, but generally it follows this pattern:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"global\": {\n    \"model\": {\n      \"primary\": \"openrouter/stepfun/step-3.5-flash:free\",\n      \"fallback\": null\n    },\n    \"provider\": \"openrouter\",\n    \"api_key\": null\n  },\n  \n  \"agents\": {\n    \"coder\": {\n      \"model\": {\n        \"primary\": \"anthropic/claude-3.5-sonnet:free\"\n      },\n      \"provider\": \"openrouter\",\n      \"enabled\": true\n    },\n    \n    \"social\": {\n      \"model\": {\n        \"primary\": \"openai/gpt-4o-mini:free\"\n      },\n      \"provider\": \"openrouter\",\n      \"enabled\": true\n    },\n    \n    \"osint\": {\n      \"model\": {\n        \"primary\": \"anthropic/claude-3.5-sonnet:free\"\n      },\n      \"provider\": \"openrouter\",\n      \"enabled\": true\n    },\n    \n    \"generalist\": {\n      \"model\": {\n        \"primary\": \"openrouter/stepfun/step-3.5-flash:free\"\n      },\n      \"provider\": \"openrouter\",\n      \"enabled\": true\n    }\n  },\n  \n  \"providers\": {\n    \"openrouter\": {\n      \"api_key\": \"${OPENROUTER_API_KEY}\",\n      \"base_url\": \"https://openrouter.ai/api/v1\",\n      \"headers\": {\n        \"HTTP-Referer\": \"https://openclaw.ai\",\n        \"X-Title\": \"OpenClaw Agent System\"\n      }\n    }\n  },\n  \n  \"models\": {\n    \"catalog\": {\n      \"anthropic/claude-3.5-sonnet:free\": {\n        \"context_window\": 200000,\n        \"max_tokens\": 4096,\n        \"cost_per_1k_input\": 0.0,\n        \"cost_per_1k_output\": 0.0,\n        \"provider\": \"openrouter\"\n      },\n      \"openai/gpt-4o-mini:free\": {\n        \"context_window\": 128000,\n        \"max_tokens\": 4096,\n        \"cost_per_1k_input\": 0.0,\n        \"cost_per_1k_output\": 0.0,\n        \"provider\": \"openrouter\"\n      },\n      \"openrouter/stepfun/step-3.5-flash:free\": {\n        \"context_window\": 32000,\n        \"max_tokens\": 4096,\n        \"cost_per_1k_input\": 0.0,\n        \"cost_per_1k_output\": 0.0,\n        \"provider\": \"openrouter\"\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n### Key Configuration Notes:\n\n1. **Agent-specific overrides**: Place `model.primary` inside each agent's config\n2. **Global fallback**: If an agent-specific model fails, it falls back to `global.defaults.model.primary`\n3. **Provider API keys**: Store in environment variables, not in JSON (use `${VAR_NAME}` syntax)\n4. **Model catalog**: Optional but useful for tracking capabilities and costs\n\n### Provider Authentication\n\nSet environment variables:\n```bash\n# For OpenRouter (all models in this plan)\nexport OPENROUTER_API_KEY=\"sk-or-v1-xxxxxxxxx\"\n\n# If using multiple providers in future\n# export ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=\"sk-ant-...\"\n# export OPENAI_API_KEY=\"sk-...\"\n```\n\n---\n\n## 4. Migration Steps\n\n### Phase 0: Preparation (Day 1)\n**Duration**: 2-4 hours\n\n**Tasks**:\n1. **Backup current configuration**\n   ```bash\n   cp openclaw.json openclaw.json.backup.2025-02-17\n   ```\n\n2. **Create new configuration file** with per-agent model settings\n   - Use the structure above as template\n   - Set all agents to new models but **keep them disabled initially**\n   - Or set `enabled: false` for all specialized agents\n\n3. **Test API connectivity** for each provider\n   ```bash\n   # Verify OpenRouter key works\n   curl -H \"Authorization: Bearer $OPENROUTER_API_KEY\" \\\n        https://openrouter.ai/api/v1/models\n   ```\n\n4. **Update environment** with API keys if not already set\n\n5. **Document rollback procedure** clearly:\n   - Command to restore backup: `cp openclaw.json.backup.2025-02-17 openclaw.json`\n   - Restart OpenClaw: `openclaw restart` (or appropriate command)\n\n---\n\n### Phase 1: Dry Run - Configure Only (Day 1-2)\n**Duration**: 24 hours\n\n**Actions**:\n1. Deploy updated `openclaw.json` but **do not switch agents yet**\n   - Keep all agents using global default model\n   - New models present in config but not active\n\n2. **Validate configuration syntax**:\n   ```bash\n   openclaw config validate\n   # or restart and check logs for config errors\n   ```\n\n3. **Monitor for errors** in logs (but no functional change yet)\n\n4. **Prepare monitoring dashboards**:\n   - Set up cost tracking (see Section 5)\n   - Create performance metrics baseline\n\n---\n\n### Phase 2: Pilot - Switch Generalist Agent (Day 3)\n**Duration**: 1 week\n\n**Rationale**: Generalist has lowest risk; uses fast, free model\n\n**Actions**:\n1. Enable Generalist agent's dedicated model:\n   ```json\n   \"generalist\": {\n     \"model\": { \"primary\": \"openrouter/stepfun/step-3.5-flash:free\" },\n     \"provider\": \"openrouter\",\n     \"enabled\": true\n   }\n   ```\n\n2. **Monitor closely**:\n   - Error rates in logs\n   - Response times (should be similar to before)\n   - Cost: Should remain $0 with free tier\n   - User feedback: Any degradation in quality?\n\n3. **Success criteria**:\n   - No increase in errors\n   - Response times acceptable (<2s typical)\n   - No user complaints\n   - No unexpected costs\n\n4. **Rollback trigger**: If any critical issue, revert Generalist to global default immediately\n\n---\n\n### Phase 3: Scale - Switch Remaining Agents (Week 2)\n**Duration**: 1-2 weeks (staggered)\n\n#### Day 1: Switch Coder Agent\n- Enable: `\"primary\": \"anthropic/claude-3.5-sonnet:free\"`\n- Monitor: Code quality metrics (if measurable), response times, error rates\n- Note: Free tier rate limits may be hit; watch for 429 errors\n- Fallback: If rate limited too much, switch to paid tier or alternative model\n\n#### Day 4: Switch Social Media Agent\n- Enable: `\"primary\": \"openai/gpt-4o-mini:free\"`\n- Monitor: Content quality, tone adaptation, user engagement feedback\n- Watch for: Hallucinations in hashtags/trends, platform policy violations\n\n#### Day 7: Switch OSINT Analyst Agent\n- Enable: `\"primary\": \"anthropic/claude-3.5-sonnet:free\"`\n- Monitor: Fact-check accuracy, source citation quality, report completeness\n- Critical: Track hallucination rate (compare to ground truth if possible)\n\n---\n\n### Phase 4: Optimization (Week 3-4)\n**Duration**: Ongoing\n\n**Tasks**:\n1. **Collect metrics** on all agents:\n   - Cost per agent (if using paid tiers)\n   - Average response times\n   - Success rates\n   - User satisfaction scores\n\n2. **Adjust if needed**:\n   - If free tier rate limits are problematic, upgrade specific agents to paid\n   - If quality insufficient, try alternative models from catalog\n   - Fine-tune context window settings per agent\n\n3. **Document final configuration** as the new standard\n\n---\n\n## 5. Cost Tracking\n\n### Setup Cost Monitoring\n\nOpenClaw typically integrates with provider billing APIs or uses token counting. Implement per-agent cost tracking:\n\n#### Option A: Token Logging + Manual Calculation\n1. **Enable detailed logging** in OpenClaw:\n   ```json\n   \"logging\": {\n     \"token_usage\": true,\n     \"per_agent\": true,\n     \"file\": \"logs/token-usage.jsonl\"\n   }\n   ```\n\n2. **Parse logs daily** to aggregate usage by agent:\n   ```bash\n   # Example script\n   jq -r 'select(.agent == \"coder\") | .input_tokens, .output_tokens' logs/token-usage.jsonl | \\\n     awk '{sum+=$1} END {print \"Coder tokens:\", sum}'\n   ```\n\n3. **Calculate costs** using model pricing from provider catalog:\n   ```\n   Cost = (input_tokens * cost_per_1k_input + output_tokens * cost_per_1k_output) / 1000\n   ```\n\n#### Option B: Provider Dashboard Monitoring\n- **OpenRouter Dashboard**: https://openrouter.ai/account/usage\n  - Shows per-model spend\n  - Map models to agents manually\n  - Update daily/weekly\n\n- **Set up alerts**:\n  - 50% of monthly budget reached\n  - Daily spend spike detection\n  - Per-agent usage anomalies\n\n#### Cost Tracking Spreadsheet Template\n\n| Agent | Model | Input Tokens/Day | Output Tokens/Day | Cost/Day | Cost/Month |\n|-------|-------|------------------|-------------------|----------|------------|\n| Coder | Claude Sonnet | 500K | 200K | $X.XX | $Y.YY |\n| Social | GPT-4o Mini | 300K | 100K | $A.AA | $B.BB |\n| OSINT | Claude Sonnet | 400K | 150K | $X.XX | $Y.YY |\n| Generalist | Step Flash | 100K | 50K | $0.00 | $0.00 |\n| **Total** | | 1.3M | 500K | **$Total** | **$Monthly** |\n\n**Note**: Free tier models show $0 cost, but watch for rate limit warnings that may require upgrade.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Risks & Mitigations\n\n### Risk 1: Provider Outage\n**Impact**: Agent becomes completely non-functional\n\n**Mitigation**:\n- **Per-agent fallback models** in configuration:\n  ```json\n  \"coder\": {\n    \"model\": {\n      \"primary\": \"anthropic/claude-3.5-sonnet:free\",\n      \"fallback\": \"openrouter/meta-llama/llama-3.1-405b-instruct:free\"\n    }\n  }\n  ```\n- **Global default**: Set to a reliable free model as ultimate fallback\n- **Health checks**: Implement periodic connectivity tests to each provider\n- **Monitoring**: Alert on sustained API errors (>5 min)\n\n---\n\n### Risk 2: API Key Rotation / Compromise\n**Impact**: Authentication failures, potential security issue\n\n**Mitigation**:\n- **Store keys in environment**, not in config files\n- **Use secret manager** in production (HashiCorp Vault, AWS Secrets Manager, etc.)\n- **Rotate keys periodically** (quarterly)\n- **Monitor key usage** for anomalies (unexpected spikes)\n- **Document recovery procedure**: How to quickly update keys without downtime\n\n---\n\n### Risk 3: Rate Limiting / Quota Exhaustion (Free Tiers)\n**Impact**: Reduced availability, delayed responses\n\n**Mitigation**:\n- **Monitor 429 responses** in logs\n- **Implement exponential backoff** in OpenClaw's retry logic (if configurable)\n- **Set up alerts** when usage reaches 80% of estimated free quota\n- **Paid upgrade plan ready**: Have paid API keys on standby for quick switch\n- **Distribute load**: Use different free models for same agent type if one hits limit\n\n---\n\n### Risk 4: Model Quality Degradation\n**Impact**: Poor outputs, user dissatisfaction\n\n**Mitigation**:\n- **A/B testing**: Run old vs new model in parallel for comparison (if feasible)\n- **Quality metrics**: Track error rates, user corrections, satisfaction scores\n- **Feedback loop**: Log quality issues with model version\n- **Quick rollback**: Keep backup config; can switch back in <5 minutes\n- **Gradual rollout**: Phase approach (Generalist first) limits blast radius\n\n---\n\n### Risk 5: Cost Overrun (Paid Tiers)\n**Impact**: Unexpected high bills\n\n**Mitigation**:\n- **Hard spending limits** in provider accounts (OpenRouter allows setting monthly budget)\n- **Per-agent quotas** in OpenClaw config:\n  ```json\n  \"coder\": {\n    \"limits\": {\n      \"max_tokens_per_day\": 1000000,\n      \"max_requests_per_day\": 500\n    }\n  }\n  ```\n- **Daily cost reports**: Automated summary via email or channel notification\n- **Alerts at thresholds**: 25%, 50%, 75%, 100% of budget\n- **Separate API keys per agent**: Enables per-agent spending caps at provider level\n\n---\n\n### Risk 6: Context Window Mismatches\n**Impact**: Truncated prompts, lost information\n\n**Mitigation**:\n- **Test each model's context limits** before deployment\n- **Configure per-agent context management** (chunking, summarization strategies)\n- **Log context usage**: Track how much of window is consumed\n- **Adjust agent prompts** to be more concise if needed\n\n---\n\n### Risk 7: Multimodal Capability Gaps\n**Impact**: Agents fail on image-based tasks\n\n**Mitigation**:\n- **Verify model capabilities** before assignment:\n  - GPT-4o Mini: Multimodal ✓\n  - Claude 3.5 Sonnet: Multimodal ✓\n  - Step-3.5 Flash: Likely text-only ✗\n- **If multimodal needed**, add specialized vision model as fallback\n- **Or route social agent** to multimodal-capable model if image understanding is critical\n\n---\n\n## 7. Timeline Summary\n\n| Phase | Duration | Actions | Success Criteria |\n|-------|----------|---------|------------------|\n| **Preparation** | Day 1 | Backup, config draft, API test | Config validated, rollback tested |\n| **Dry Run** | Day 1-2 | Deploy config, no agent switch | No config errors, monitoring ready |\n| **Pilot** | Day 3-9 | Switch Generalist only | Zero critical issues, acceptable performance |\n| **Scale** | Week 2 | Switch Coder, Social, OSINT (staggered) | All agents functional, quality acceptable |\n| **Optimization** | Week 3-4 | Monitor, adjust, document | Costs controlled, user satisfaction maintained |\n\n**Total duration**: 3-4 weeks end-to-end\n\n---\n\n## 8. Configuration Quick Reference\n\n### Final `openclaw.json` Summary\n\n```json\n{\n  \"global\": {\n    \"model\": { \"primary\": \"openrouter/stepfun/step-3.5-flash:free\" },\n    \"provider\": \"openrouter\"\n  },\n  \n  \"agents\": {\n    \"coder\": {\n      \"model\": { \"primary\": \"anthropic/claude-3.5-sonnet:free\" },\n      \"provider\": \"openrouter\",\n      \"enabled\": true\n    },\n    \"social\": {\n      \"model\": { \"primary\": \"openai/gpt-4o-mini:free\" },\n      \"provider\": \"openrouter\",\n      \"enabled\": true\n    },\n    \"osint\": {\n      \"model\": { \"primary\": \"anthropic/claude-3.5-sonnet:free\" },\n      \"provider\": \"openrouter\",\n      \"enabled\": true\n    },\n    \"generalist\": {\n      \"model\": { \"primary\": \"openrouter/stepfun/step-3.5-flash:free\" },\n      \"provider\": \"openrouter\",\n      \"enabled\": true\n    }\n  },\n  \n  \"providers\": {\n    \"openrouter\": {\n      \"api_key\": \"${OPENROUTER_API_KEY}\",\n      \"base_url\": \"https://openrouter.ai/api/v1\"\n    }\n  },\n  \n  \"monitoring\": {\n    \"token_logging\": true,\n    \"cost_alerts\": true,\n    \"daily_report_channel\": \"#agent-costs\"\n  }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## 9. Post-Migration Checklist\n\n- [ ] All agents operational with new models\n- [ ] No increase in error rates (>5% threshold)\n- [ ] Response times within acceptable ranges:\n  - Coder: <5s for simple tasks, <15s for complex\n  - Social: <3s\n  - OSINT: <10s\n  - Generalist: <2s\n- [ ] Cost tracking active and accurate\n- [ ] Alerts configured and tested\n- [ ] Documentation updated (README, internal wiki)\n- [ ] Team trained on rollback procedures\n- [ ] Quarterly review scheduled to reassess model choices\n\n---\n\n## 10. Conclusion & Recommendations\n\n### Recommended Model Allocation:\n\n| Agent | Model | Provider | Cost Tier | Reasoning |\n|-------|-------|----------|-----------|-----------|\n| **Coder** | Claude 3.5 Sonnet | OpenRouter | Free→Paid | Best code quality, large context |\n| **Social** | GPT-4o Mini | OpenRouter | Free→Paid | Creative, fast, multimodal |\n| **OSINT** | Claude 3.5 Sonnet | OpenRouter | Free→Paid | Superior reasoning, accuracy |\n| **Generalist** | Step-3.5 Flash | OpenRouter | Free | Cost-effective, adequate capability |\n\n### Key Takeaways:\n\n1. **Don't keep all agents on the same model** - specialization improves quality and cost efficiency\n2. **Phase the migration** - pilot with Generalist, then scale carefully\n3. **Monitor relentlessly** - cost, quality, availability metrics are essential\n4. **Plan for failure** - fallbacks, rollbacks, and alerts must be in place\n5. **OpenRouter is the best provider** for this multi-model strategy due to unified API and free tier access\n\n### Next Steps:\n\n1. Review this plan with stakeholders\n2. Set up environment variables for API keys\n3. Create `openclaw.json` with Phase 0 approach (disabled agents)\n4. Begin migration following the phased timeline\n5. Schedule weekly review meetings during transition period\n\n---\n\n**Document Version**: 1.0  \n**Last Updated**: 2025-02-17  \n**Owner**: OpenClaw Configuration Team\n","file_path":"D:\\workspace\\appDev\\openclaw\\workspace-generalist\\agent-model-assignment-plan.md"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"openrouter","model":"stepfun/step-3.5-flash:free","usage":{"input":13600,"output":5975,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":19575,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1771347952700}}
